This is a complete guide to building a **CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete)** application using React (Frontend) and Node.js with Express (Backend).

I will structure this into two parts:
1.  **Development:** How to build the app with dummy data.
2.  **Deployment:** How to build the code and deploy it to a cloud server (Vercel for React, Render/Heroku for Node, or a VPS).

---

# Part 1: Building the App

### Prerequisites
*   Node.js installed.
*   Basic terminal knowledge.

### Step 1: Setup Backend (Node/Express)

1.  Create a folder named `my-app`.
2.  Open terminal inside it and create the backend:

```bash
mkdir backend
cd backend
npm init -y
npm install express cors body-parser
```

3.  Create a file named `server.js`:

```javascript
// backend/server.js
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
const path = require('path');

const app = express();
const PORT = 5000;

// Middleware
app.use(cors()); // Allow React to talk to Node
app.use(bodyParser.json());

// --- DUMMY DATA ---
const dummyItems = [
    { id: 1, name: "Task 1", status: "Pending" },
    { id: 2, name: "Task 2", status: "Completed" },
    { id: 3, name: "Task 3", status: "Pending" },
];

// --- API ROUTES ---

// 1. GET all items
app.get('/api/items', (req, res) => {
    res.json(dummyItems);
});

// 2. POST create item
app.post('/api/items', (req, res) => {
    const newItem = {
        id: Date.now(), // simple ID generation
        name: req.body.name,
        status: req.body.status
    };
    dummyItems.push(newItem);
    res.status(201).json(newItem);
});

// 3. DELETE item
app.delete('/api/items/:id', (req, res) => {
    const id = parseInt(req.params.id);
    const index = dummyItems.findIndex(item => item.id === id);

    if (index > -1) {
        dummyItems.splice(index, 1);
        res.json({ message: "Deleted successfully" });
    } else {
        res.status(404).json({ message: "Item not found" });
    }
});

// Serve static files if we were using a real DB, 
// but here we just run the API on port 5000
app.listen(PORT, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);
});
```

